0 < 2 # Fold vs serialize on the One Driver — 2026-08-30
1 < 2 
2 < 2 The registered A/B protocol, re-run on the 0.3.0 engine with the rebuilt rig
3 < 2 (#402 item 6). Twelve cells across six fixtures, both arms, serial, local
4 < 2 (`evals/ab_runner.py`, laptop subscription). Engine ref `eca7dd9`.
5 < 2 Rows are appended to `evals/results/runs.jsonl`; the `wide` pair comes from the
6 < 2 same day's operator smoke.
7 < 2 
8 < 2 ## Result
9 < 2 
10 < 2 | fixture | fold | serialize | ratio | fold waves | ser. waves | fold tok | ser. tok | tok ratio |
11 < 2 |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
12 < 2 | wide | 201.5s | 207.7s | 0.970 | 1 | 1 | 32,287 | 32,610 | 0.990 |
13 < 2 | chained | 512.7s | 537.3s | 0.954 | 5 | 5 | 37,942 | 39,230 | 0.967 |
14 < 2 | mixed | 457.5s | 453.7s | 1.008 | 4 | 4 | 43,446 | 42,537 | 1.021 |
15 < 2 | contend | 215.8s | 277.3s | 0.778 | 1 | 3 | 27,659 | 22,334 | 1.238 |
16 < 2 | contend-prod | 979.5s | 1468.7s | 0.667 | 1 | 4 | 186,275 | 132,597 | 1.405 |
17 < 2 | contend-big | 934.0s | 2611.3s | **0.358** | 1 | 4 | 212,310 | 227,456 | 0.933 |
18 < 2 
19 < 2 - **Aggregate wall: 0.594×** (3301s fold vs 5556s serialize) — the registered
20 < 2   2026-08-14 baseline was 0.640× on the pre-cutover engine. Fold's advantage
21 < 2   survives the One Driver cutover intact.
22 < 2 - **Contention-only wall: 0.489×** — over the three fixtures whose wave shape
23 < 2   actually differs between arms, fold runs in half the time.
24 < 2 - **Aggregate output tokens: 1.087×** — fold costs ~9% more tokens for that
25 < 2   wall-clock win. Per-fixture the token penalty concentrates where contention
26 < 2   is resolved (`contend-prod` 1.41×, `contend` 1.24×), and `contend-big`
27 < 2   inverts (0.93×) — a long serialize chain re-reads more context than a
28 < 2   resolved fold does.
29 < 2 
30 < 2 ## What the shape column proves
31 < 2 
32 < 2 The three non-contention fixtures compile to **identical wave shapes in both
33 < 2 arms** (wide 1/1, chained 5/5, mixed 4/4). With the same shape the two cells
34 < 2 run the same work in the same order, so their ratios — 0.970, 0.954, 1.008 —
35 < 2 measure nothing but run-to-run LLM latency variance. That is the noise floor:
36 < 2 **±5%**. Every contention ratio (0.778, 0.667, 0.358) clears it by a wide
37 < 2 margin.
38 < 2 
39 < 2 This doubles as the arm-identity check the old rig enforced explicitly: the
40 < 2 serialize arm really does serialize (3–4 waves where fold takes 1), so the
41 < 2 protocol is comparing two genuinely different execution geometries rather than
42 < 2 running the same thing twice.
43 < 2 
44 < 2 ## Caveat on comparability
45 < 2 
46 < 2 0.640× (2026-08-14) was measured on the deleted engine with a different worker
47 < 2 mix; 0.594× here is a *different measurement*, not a regression or improvement
48 < 2 of that number. What this run establishes cleanly is fold-vs-serialize **on
49 < 2 today's engine**, which is the decision-relevant comparison — the default is
50 < 2 already fold, and it stays justified.
51 < 2 
52 < 2 ## Excluded fixtures
53 < 2 
54 < 2 - `flawed`, `flawed-routing`, `degrade` — deliberate-defect fixtures that
55 < 2   exercise the compiler's diagnostics; their historical arm rows came back
56 < 2   `invalid` on arm identity.
57 < 2 - `webapp` — greenfield fixture: its project tree is `package.json` + README,
58 < 2   and the plan creates the tests, so no baseline suite can be green at BASE.
59 < 2   Knob validation refuses (correctly, in 0.3s for $0.00) under both
60 < 2   `python3 -m pytest` and `npm test`. It was never in the registered A/B set
61 < 2   (historical rows carry `arm=None`).
62 < 2 
63 < 2 ## Run conditions
64 < 2 
65 < 2 All fourteen cells ended `gate-blocked` on the same `deferred:manual` ack — the
66 < 2 TDD red-then-green ordering, which is process-order and not observable in a
67 < 2 diff. That is the honest verdict for an unattended cell and does not affect the
68 < 2 timing or token measurement. Total spend $69.85.
